  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122070,
      "SummaryText": "The BASF Corporation Bishop facility (BASF Bishop) is a manufacturing site for chemicals and pharmaceuticals, located in a rural area between Kingsville and Bishop, Texas. BASF Bishop shares the location with two other companies: OXEA Bishop LLC, Celanese Chemicals, Inc./ Ticona Polymers, Inc. These companies own and operate assets and manage RMP programs at the location, independently from BASF Bishop.\n\nBASF Bishop is the owner/operator of RMP-covered processes using anhydrous hydrogen fluoride (HF) and hydrochloric acid (HCl). This is a resubmittal for the HF stored and used in the 4-hydroxyacetophenone (4-HAP) process area and IBU - Acylation process; and addition of HCl stored and used in the Carbonylation area.\n\nThe facility processes covered under both EPA's RMP and OSHA's process safety management (PSM) standards; and are currently managed as part of the facility's Responsible Care Management System (RCMS). BASF's RCMS comprises the global rules, standards and procedures for environmental and health protection, safety, and security and forms the superordinate structure for the EHS documents. This document applies throughout the BASF Group and covers all EHS topics including RMP/PSM. In addition, the facility's processes are ISO 9001 certified.\n\nHF and HCl are used at this facility in the manufacturing of chemicals and pharmaceuticals (generally ibuprofen, 4-hydroxyacetophenone (4-HAP), and aluminum trilactate).   Approximately 133,000 lb. of HF is present at the facility, in the processes and storage tank.  HF is delivered by a tanker truck into the facility's HF storage tank.  \n5\nApproximately 13,000 gallons of 38% HCl is covered under RMP Program 3 from the HCl storage vessel to the HCl charge vessel, through the feed pumps.  As soon as HCl gets into the reactors its concentration gets diluted to <30% due to residual HCL.\n\nThe information submitted in the RMP includes accident prevention and safety programs, emergency preparedness and response programs, che"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122070,
      "SummaryText": "mical descriptions, five-year accidental history. BASF Bishop has not had any releases of HCl or HF that resulted in, or could reasonably have resulted in a catastrophic release, since BASF Bishop's acquisition in January 2017. Worst-case scenarios (WCS) and alternate release scenarios (ARS) were prepared for each covered process bae on the results of the current PHAs for each process.\n\nProcess safety is a core part of BASF Bishop's safe and efficient production. BASF Bishop follows corporate values and never compromises on safety in the facility operation. In addition to the RMP WCS and ARS, BASF uses a risk matrix to assess the risks arising from potential incident based on their probability and impact. The resulting risk classes are used to determine appropriate protection measures, which are divided in so-called primary and secondary protection measures. Primary protection measures prevent further consequences such as the release of substances in the event of a plant malfunction. To this end, equipment is installed to shut down the plant automatically or safety valves are used to prevent overpressure. Our focus lies on these primary protection measures. Secondary protection measures back up the primary measures. They include protection walls, enclosures, retention systems and firefighting measures designed to restrict the impact of an incident to a manageable level.\n\nBASF Bishop has an incident reporting system designed to allow the recording of security-relevant incidents which endanger or harm BASF, its employees, facilities, products or information, including RMP-related incidents. Incident reporting will be facilitated by means of a single, standardized database that provides Corporate Security with optimal access to all incidents. The timely evaluation of this information will allow for the early detection of security tendencies, along with the implementation of appropriate countermeasures and investigative procedures. The BASF Bishop location shares and co"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000122070,
      "SummaryText": "ordinates security and emergency response services with OXEA Bishop LLC and Celanese Chemicals, Inc. The location has an on-site emergency response team which would initially respond to an incident with backup support from the local emergency response agencies (LEPC) as necessary. Emergency response drills are routinely conducted for BASF employees as well as location-wide drills with the on-site emergency response team. In addition, the BASF Corporation has a global Emergency Response Management System that has the comprehensive knowledge and experience to handle both on-site and off-site incidents.\n\nIn 2018 BASF Bishop commenced a comprehensive mechanical integrity inspection of its processes. BASF Bishop implementation of recommended preventative actions identified during the inspection is on-going to improve to the safety of its processes."
    }
  ]
